\title{distributionalDensity: Prevalence, Burstiness, Position, and Dispersion of Verbal Behavior}
\description{
Computes the four distributional density parameters of a psychologically-consequential language dimension within a text: prevalence (how much of the dimension is present), burstiness (how clustered its occurrences are, using the finite-size-corrected estimator of Kim and Jo (2016) \doi{10.1103/PhysRevE.94.032311} built on Goh and Barabasi (2008) \doi{10.1209/0295-5075/81/48002}), position (the mean normalized placement of occurrences in the text), and dispersion (the spread of occurrences around their mean position, normalized by the maximum attainable spread at that mean). Together these parameters describe how a verbal category is distributed across a disclosure rather than only how frequently it occurs, extending prevalence-based text analysis with a within-text analogue of Whole Trait Theory's density distributions (Fleeson, 2001) \doi{10.1037/0022-3514.80.6.1011}.
}
